WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.170 --> 00:01.730
So how do we arrive at a recommendation?

2
00:02.150 --> 00:06.470
At this point in the process, we've gone through
the first three steps of the hypothesis-led approach.

3
00:06.850 --> 00:12.236
We've defined the problem, we've structured the problem,
and we've prioritised the issues.

4
00:12.236 --> 00:14.750
And now it's time for the last four steps.

5
00:15.240 --> 00:18.910
So now we're going to think about how we plan
the analysis, and how we conduct the analysis —

6
00:19.490 --> 00:24.558
how we arrive at a synthesis of what we've done,
and ultimately drive to a recommendation.

7
00:24.558 --> 00:26.698
Let's spend a moment on each of those.

8
00:26.698 --> 00:33.400
So let's talk about planning the analysis. When you think
about the work to be done, start with the prioritised issues.

9
00:33.540 --> 00:38.496
What is your hypothesis against each of those?
What are you trying to prove or disprove?

10
00:38.496 --> 00:41.707
What analysis do you need to conduct in order to do that?

11
00:41.707 --> 00:47.113
And then, ultimately, think about accountability:
who is going to do that analysis, and by when?

12
00:47.113 --> 00:49.310
And one of the challenges we see here —

13
00:49.510 --> 00:57.339
is thinking about the interdependencies, solving for the timing,
and making sure each analysis is discrete enough to be tackled properly.

14
00:57.339 --> 01:02.483
Now, conducting the analysis: this will vary
depending on the type of analysis being done.

15
01:02.483 --> 01:05.340
It could be qualitative. It could be quantitative.

16
01:05.610 --> 01:09.380
But there are a couple of things you want
to think about to do it well.

17
01:09.860 --> 01:12.339
It's not just “are we busy?” — it's “what are we busy about?”

18
01:12.339 --> 01:15.590
How is this helping us drive to the answer
to the problem we're trying to solve?

19
01:15.970 --> 01:21.381
And when we do the analysis, we want to think about
how we can be as efficient as possible.

20
01:21.381 --> 01:27.863
Can we get to the 80/20 solution and save time,
or do we need to go all the way to the exact, precise answer?

21
01:27.863 --> 01:31.550
And the last point on analysis is
to constantly ask yourself —

22
01:32.130 --> 01:34.891
is this analysis helping us get to the “so what”?

23
01:34.891 --> 01:40.130
Which leads us to the next step in the problem-solving
process, which is all about synthesis.

24
01:40.550 --> 01:45.250
I often ask myself: what is the difference between
a synthesis and a summary — and which one is better?

25
01:45.890 --> 01:50.404
Well, a summary is simply a chronological recount
of everything that you've done.

26
01:50.404 --> 01:55.810
A synthesis, on the other hand, is an integrated
“so what” of everything you've done in the work.

27
01:56.200 --> 01:59.170
When you have the synthesis,
you're almost done — but not quite,

28
01:59.560 --> 02:02.630
because the synthesis is simply
the outcome of the “so what”.

29
02:03.170 --> 02:09.790
A recommendation is what you actually need to do
to achieve the value creation, or the impact, of the work.

30
02:10.280 --> 02:18.530
Put differently: think about your recommendation as the way
you mobilise and pragmatically implement your strategy.

31
02:19.110 --> 02:21.651
There are many ways to think about this.

32
02:21.651 --> 02:29.021
For example, we often use the influence model to think about
all the pieces your strategy — or your recommendation —

33
02:29.021 --> 02:34.040
needs to dock into, so that you're able to realise the impact.
Be mindful here.

34
02:34.760 --> 02:38.980
Getting the organisation to align and mobilise
is half of the battle.

35
02:39.330 --> 02:45.027
You may have the most elegant solution, but without buy-in,
without the conviction of the organisation,

36
02:45.027 --> 02:48.290
you may not achieve the full impact
you initially intended.

37
02:48.680 --> 02:52.390
So one of the challenges related to that is:
have you thought about —

38
02:53.540 --> 02:57.067
de-biasing some of the challenges — the confirmation bias, for one.

39
02:57.067 --> 03:02.542
If you get to a “so what” in the first week of a project,
how do you make sure you're still open-minded,

40
03:02.542 --> 03:07.490
challenging it to get to the right solution overall —
not just the right answer from week one?
